WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:04.670
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:10.940
Today, it is possible to teach virtual characters
to perform highly dynamic motions, like a

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.090
cartwheel or backflips.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:19.970
And not only that, but we can teach an AI
to perform this differently from other characters,

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.890
to do it with style if you will.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:23.890
But!

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.020
Today, we are not looking to be stylish.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.150
Today, we are looking to be efficient!

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:34.929
In this paper, researchers placed an AI in
a physics simulation and asked it to control

00:00:34.929 --> 00:00:41.300
a virtual character and gave it one task:
to jump as high as it can.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:47.449
And when I heard this idea, I was elated,
and immediately wondered - did it come up

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:51.129
with popular techniques that exist in the
real world?

00:00:51.129 --> 00:00:54.460
Well, let’s see…yes!

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.569
Woo-hoo!

00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:58.280
That is indeed a Fosbury flop.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.799
This allows the athlete to jump backward over
the bar, thus lowering their center of gravity.

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:09.920
Even today, this is the prevalent technique
in high jump competitions.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.890
With this technique, the takeoff takes place
relatively late.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:20.140
The only problem is that the AI didn’t clear
the bar so far…so, can it?

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.970
Well, this is a learning-based algorithm,
so, with a little more practice, it should

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:32.039
improve - yes, great work!

00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:38.140
If we lower the bar just a tiny bit for this
virtual athlete, we can also observe it performing

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.030
the western roll.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:46.140
With this technique, we take off a little
earlier and we don’t jump backward, but

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.290
sideways.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.929
If it had nothing else, this would already
be a great paper, but we are not nearly done

00:01:52.929 --> 00:01:53.929
yet.

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:56.080
The best is yet to come!

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.619
This is a simulation.

00:01:57.619 --> 00:02:02.920
A virtual world if you will, and here, we
make all the rules.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.600
The limit is only our imagination.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.940
The authors know that very well and you will
see that they indeed have a very vivid imagination.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:17.501
For instance, we can also simulate a jump
with a weak take-off leg and see that with

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:24.120
this condition, the little AI can only clear
a bar that is approximately one foot lower

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.630
than its previous record.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:32.100
What about another virtual athlete with an
inflexible spine?

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.950
It can jump approximately two feet lower.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.680
Here is the difference compared to the original.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.709
I am enjoying this a great deal, and it’s
only getting better.

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:52.840
Next, what happens if we are injured and have
a cast on the take-off knee?

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.620
What results can we expect?

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.080
Something like this.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.549
We can jump a little more than two feet lower.

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:04.220
What about organizing the olympics on Mars?

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:05.819
What would that look like?

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:08.950
What would the world record be with the weaker
gravity there?

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:17.000
Well, hold on to your papers, and look…
yes, we could jump three feet higher than

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:26.540
on earth, and then…ouch, well missed the
foam matting, but otherwise, very impressive.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.239
And if we are already there, why limit the
simulation to high jumps?

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:33.379
Why not try something else?

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:36.790
Again, in a simulation, we can do anything!

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:42.860
Previously, the task was to jump over the
bar, but we can also recreate the simulation

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:47.090
to include instead, jumping through obstacles.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.640
To get all of these magical results, the authors
propose a step they call “Bayesian Diversity

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:53.710
Search”.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:59.270
This helps systematically creating a rich
selection of novel strategies, and it does

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.170
this efficiently.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:07.829
The authors also went the extra mile and included
a comparison to motion capture footage performed

00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:09.560
by a real athlete.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.719
But note that the AI’s version uses a similar
technique and is able to clear a significantly

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:19.880
higher bar without ever seeing a high jump
move.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:25.540
The method was trained on motion capture footage
to get used to humanlike movements, like walking,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:31.190
running, and kicks, but it has never seen
any high jump techniques before.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.200
Wow.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:38.820
So, if this can invent high jumping techniques
that took decades for humans to invent, I

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.380
wonder what else it could invent?

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.410
What do you think?

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.440
Let me know in the comments below!

00:04:44.440 --> 00:05:30.610
What a time to be alive!

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:38.210
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.130
and I'll see you next time!

